WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.140
Instructor: While it is lovely

00:01.140 --> 00:02.749
being able to initialize a new repo,

00:02.749 --> 00:04.009
we still don't know how to do anything

00:04.009 --> 00:05.690
inside of those repos.

00:05.690 --> 00:07.720
But that's gonna change because we're about to talk about

00:07.720 --> 00:10.219
the most fundamental Git workflow,

00:10.219 --> 00:13.478
the bread and butter of Git, which is committing.

00:13.478 --> 00:14.964
So just like before, we're gonna start with

00:14.964 --> 00:17.219
a quick high-level conceptual overview

00:17.219 --> 00:19.685
and intro to the concept of committing,

00:19.685 --> 00:21.690
which is what this video is.

00:21.690 --> 00:23.850
And then I'll show you the associated commands.

00:23.850 --> 00:25.176
There's two that we need to talk about

00:25.176 --> 00:26.685
in the next couple of videos.

00:26.685 --> 00:29.747
So let's begin by answering a simple question.

00:29.747 --> 00:33.540
What is committing? Or, what is a Git commit?

00:33.540 --> 00:35.744
Well, early in this course, I showed these slides.

00:35.744 --> 00:38.670
I spent kind of a long time making them.

00:38.670 --> 00:40.500
On the top right hand corner,

00:40.500 --> 00:41.668
we have a website that I'm building.

00:41.668 --> 00:44.443
And you can see as I progress, feature by feature

00:44.443 --> 00:46.763
I'm building this site out and modifying it.

00:46.763 --> 00:49.712
And then, as I go, as I make some progress,

00:49.712 --> 00:51.741
I'm adding these checkpoints.

00:51.741 --> 00:54.764
That was a term I used when I first introduced these.

00:54.764 --> 00:57.517
A checkpoint, a snapshot of my project

00:57.517 --> 01:00.760
and its new changes at a moment in time.

01:00.760 --> 01:01.978
Along with a little message,

01:01.978 --> 01:05.593
like Add Top Navbar, or Add First Row of content,

01:05.593 --> 01:08.577
or finish Bottom Row of content. Change to dark theme.

01:08.577 --> 01:10.757
Well, each one of these checkpoints

01:10.757 --> 01:13.767
is actually in Git, what we would call a commit.

01:13.767 --> 01:16.108
I just used the term checkpoint early on

01:16.108 --> 01:17.745
before we actually start learning Git,

01:17.745 --> 01:19.738
because it actually makes sense on its own.

01:19.738 --> 01:21.726
A commit doesn't really sound like much,

01:21.726 --> 01:22.977
at least in my experience.

01:22.977 --> 01:25.014
Anyway, a git-commit is one of

01:25.014 --> 01:27.746
these checkpoints in a Git repository.

01:27.746 --> 01:30.700
And typically, a single Git repository has tons

01:30.700 --> 01:33.468
and tons of these commits, one after another.

01:33.468 --> 01:35.759
And eventually, we'll learn how to go back

01:35.759 --> 01:38.689
to earlier commits, how to undo commits,

01:38.689 --> 01:41.715
how to just take a peek at an earlier commit.

01:41.715 --> 01:43.160
But for now, we're just gonna focus

01:43.160 --> 01:45.463
on making these checkpoints.

01:45.463 --> 01:48.234
How do we do some stuff in a project?

01:48.234 --> 01:50.555
How do we, you know, add a new feature to a website,

01:50.555 --> 01:51.947
and then make a commit?

01:51.947 --> 01:54.709
And then repeat. Do some more stuff, make a commit.

01:54.709 --> 01:56.942
Do some more stuff, make a commit.

01:56.942 --> 01:59.611
That is our goal for the next couple of videos.

01:59.611 --> 02:02.025
I'll quickly show you an existing repository

02:02.025 --> 02:06.519
that I have in, this is GitKraken YelpCamp 2.0.

02:06.519 --> 02:08.006
I've shown this a couple of times.

02:08.006 --> 02:10.458
What I'm looking at right here with this Gooey

02:10.458 --> 02:13.701
is a list of all of the commits that I've made in order.

02:13.701 --> 02:16.020
And actually, there's more than one contributor,

02:16.020 --> 02:16.853
so it's not just me.

02:16.853 --> 02:19.650
So you can see here's the very first commit.

02:19.650 --> 02:21.476
All the way up to some of the more recent ones.

02:21.476 --> 02:23.472
So there's what, over a hundred,

02:23.472 --> 02:26.717
maybe 150, at least, individual commits.

02:26.717 --> 02:29.006
And each one of them is a checkpoint in time,

02:29.006 --> 02:31.272
a snapshot of the repository

02:31.272 --> 02:33.725
and the new work that I did. The new changes.

02:33.725 --> 02:35.720
And it includes a little message.

02:35.720 --> 02:39.463
Like, you know, Refactor asyncErrorHandler and post methods.

02:39.463 --> 02:42.781
Or something very silly and boring, like Initial commit.

02:42.781 --> 02:47.248
Or, you know, add mapbox css back to some style sheet.

02:47.248 --> 02:49.187
Whatever that message is is up to me.

02:49.187 --> 02:50.497
But there is a message,

02:50.497 --> 02:54.271
along with this snapshot of my project in time.

02:54.271 --> 02:55.973
Now, the next thing that's crucial to understand

02:55.973 --> 02:58.749
about making a commit is that it's not the same

02:58.749 --> 03:01.413
as just saving a file.

03:01.413 --> 03:03.750
It's actually, think of it as something

03:03.750 --> 03:05.707
that's built on top of saving files.

03:05.707 --> 03:07.807
We have to make changes first,

03:07.807 --> 03:11.213
and save them to our files before we can even commit.

03:11.213 --> 03:15.718
So, we might use the term checkpoint, or save point.

03:15.718 --> 03:17.817
But we're not talking about just saving a file.

03:17.817 --> 03:19.962
That has nothing to do with Git.

03:19.962 --> 03:21.741
We save files all the time.

03:21.741 --> 03:23.758
But once we have made changes,

03:23.758 --> 03:27.258
we can then group them together into a commit.

03:27.258 --> 03:29.214
So on the right hand side, I'm showing a,

03:29.214 --> 03:32.040
potentially, a commit that I may have made,

03:32.040 --> 03:34.693
that includes changes to four different files,

03:34.693 --> 03:36.716
plus a folder called logos.

03:36.716 --> 03:40.717
And I can group that all together into a new commit.

03:40.717 --> 03:42.986
But first, I would've written some new code

03:42.986 --> 03:45.196
in one of these files and saved it.

03:45.196 --> 03:47.732
And then written more code in another one and saved it.

03:47.732 --> 03:49.693
Then I make that commit.

03:49.693 --> 03:51.151
And the next thing that you need to know

03:51.151 --> 03:52.770
about making a commit is that

03:52.770 --> 03:54.725
it's actually a multi-step process.

03:54.725 --> 03:56.908
It's not as simple as just hitting a button

03:56.908 --> 03:59.461
that says, "Commit, commit, commit."

03:59.461 --> 04:01.966
Instead, there's an intermediate step

04:01.966 --> 04:03.507
where we actually call out

04:03.507 --> 04:05.755
the particular changes that we've made

04:05.755 --> 04:07.775
that we want to include in a commit.

04:07.775 --> 04:10.702
And this allows us to selectively make commits,

04:10.702 --> 04:11.987
which is really, really nice.

04:11.987 --> 04:15.704
For example, imagine I've been working all day,

04:15.704 --> 04:18.430
or all morning, on some project.

04:18.430 --> 04:20.655
I spent a lot of time working on this project,

04:20.655 --> 04:22.993
and I made changes to seven different files.

04:22.993 --> 04:25.291
as you can see on the left hand side.

04:25.291 --> 04:27.494
I deleted a file, I modified two files,

04:27.494 --> 04:29.710
I created four files.

04:29.710 --> 04:32.474
And instead of just making one generic commit

04:32.474 --> 04:34.746
that just says checkpoint right now.

04:34.746 --> 04:38.062
Instead, I can actually call out some of these,

04:38.062 --> 04:40.253
and group them together based upon

04:40.253 --> 04:42.329
the actual stuff that I did.

04:42.329 --> 04:44.465
For example, I might group together

04:44.465 --> 04:46.453
those first three changes.

04:46.453 --> 04:49.743
I deleted a file called team.html.

04:49.743 --> 04:51.483
And then I changed two files called

04:51.483 --> 04:54.239
about.html and about.css.

04:54.239 --> 04:57.870
Those were one thing that I did across three files.

04:57.870 --> 05:00.954
I added some new team members to our about page.

05:00.954 --> 05:03.267
Whatever the, you know, about page looks like.

05:03.267 --> 05:05.056
So that was one commit.

05:05.056 --> 05:07.730
And then, I can take these other four changes,

05:07.730 --> 05:11.738
and group them together into a separate standalone commit.

05:11.738 --> 05:13.183
So this is what I'm talking about

05:13.183 --> 05:14.957
when I say there's an intermediate step.

05:14.957 --> 05:17.470
We can select certain changes,

05:17.470 --> 05:19.530
and make a commit with those changes.

05:19.530 --> 05:21.733
So we don't just say, "Commit, commit, commit."

05:21.733 --> 05:23.641
Instead, we're getting in there with tweezers,

05:23.641 --> 05:26.967
and we're saying, "All right, this, and this, and this.

05:26.967 --> 05:28.746
Now we're gonna commit those changes."

05:28.746 --> 05:30.775
So, to actually make a commit,

05:30.775 --> 05:33.520
there are two associated commands.

05:33.520 --> 05:36.500
And we're gonna talk about the first one in the next video.

05:36.500 --> 05:37.768
It's called git add.

05:37.768 --> 05:40.480
And then the second command is git commit.

05:40.480 --> 05:42.969
And we'll use git add in order to highlight,

05:42.969 --> 05:44.710
or select, those changes that we want

05:44.710 --> 05:47.701
to include in the upcoming commit.

05:47.701 --> 05:49.243
So just to reiterate all of this,

05:49.243 --> 05:51.629
a commit is a checkpoint in time.

05:51.629 --> 05:54.516
It's a snapshot of changes in our repository.

05:54.516 --> 05:55.721
Eventually, we'll be able to go back

05:55.721 --> 05:59.640
to earlier commits, undo commits, merge commits.

05:59.640 --> 06:00.770
There's a whole bunch of things that we can do.

06:00.770 --> 06:02.733
For now, we're just focused on plotting ahead

06:02.733 --> 06:04.377
and leaving a trail of commits

06:04.377 --> 06:05.967
behind a trail of checkpoints.

06:05.967 --> 06:08.524
And when we make one of these snapshots,

06:08.524 --> 06:10.510
one of these commits, we can call out

06:10.510 --> 06:13.243
the specific changes that we want to include in a commit,

06:13.243 --> 06:15.641
rather than just automatically including

06:15.641 --> 06:18.768
all changes in our project, we can select them.

06:18.768 --> 06:21.900
That's the first step. And then commit them.

06:21.900 --> 06:22.733
So in the next video,

06:22.733 --> 06:24.543
I'll show you how to do that first bit.
